  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 301 PM CST Sun Nov 8 2026\n\nAn area of low pressure was located between Lake Superior and \nJames Bay this afternoon with a cold front trailing into \nnorthern Lower Michigan and northern Illinois. High pressure was\nlocated over the Plains setting up a pressure gradient across \nthe Northland. This gradient was leading to winds gusting to 20 \nto 30 mph across the region. Heading into tonight, a ridge axis \nwill move through the Upper Midwest with winds quickly becoming \nlight and variable after sunset. Clouds will spread across the \narea overnight and keep lows slightly warmer than previously \nforecast in the middle to upper 30s.\n\nDry conditions will then prevail for Monday and Tuesday. A cold\nfront will move through Monday afternoon into Monday evening, \nbut is expected to be a dry passage with most of the energy with\nthe system lagging behind the front. Highs Monday will be in \nthe 50s before cooling into the middle 40s to middle 50s on \nTuesday behind the front.\n\nOur next chance of precipitation will be seen late Tuesday \nnight into Wednesday as an upper trough swings through the Upper\nMidwest. This will be accompanied by a midlevel lobe of \nvorticity and low pressure. With not much of a surface \nreflection and limited moisture, overall precipitation chances \nare low across the region. The exception will be across the \nSouth Shore with lake-effect rain and snow showers thanks to 850\ntemps of -5 to -10C and Lake Superior temps in the 40s F. \nMainly rain showers are expected for Wednesday before mixing \nwith and changing to snow showers overnight and lingering for \nThursday and Thursday night before drier air arrives and turns \noff the lake-effect. Any snow accumulations look to be minimal \nand limited to the northwest Wisconsin snowbelt from Ashland \nCounty into Iron County. Cooler air arrives for the latter half \nof the week with highs only in the 30s for Thursday and Friday. \nHigh pressure will then lead to dry conditions heading into the \nweekend with temperatures warming back into the 40s by Sunday.",
